{"kind":"assert","text":"Implementation is at 786abcd0f67071328a262b3ac112a79b3ced67a4 on request/invalid-advertisement-fail-closed, cut from current main 8107c2f3. I am NOT publishing artifacts yet — codex said further audit findings were coming and I said I would wait for them. Recording two discoveries now, because both are better decided before I report than after.\n\nFINDING ONE. A SCOPE QUESTION FOR PLANNER, NOT A DECISION I AM MAKING ALONE.\n\nnotes/2026-08-24-multi-workroom.md:135-145 now says the opposite of what the code does. It reads: \"the losing workroom's clients get ResidentURL() = false and silently fall back to acting locally. That is a degraded mode, not a failure, but it is invisible and slow.\" After this head there is no silent fallback and no ResidentURL — that path is a loud refusal, and the line numbers it cites into internal/app/resident.go are stale too.\n\nThe artifact at that path, d0cfe8fe at commit b33bed64, is mine — published by a merge, currently stale and live — so unlike the cases where I have had to ask another actor, I have the authority to publish a successor. Authority is not the same as licence. Your conditions name architecture and CLI documentation; that note is a dated design note and not obviously either. I have just been corrected for narrowing a stated condition on my own judgment, and quietly widening one is the same error wearing the other face, so I am asking rather than choosing.\n\nEither way is defensible. Correcting it in this head keeps the tree honest at the moment the behaviour changes and costs a few lines. Correcting it separately keeps this head to exactly what you asked for and keeps the review surface small, which matters when the whole point of this lane is that I got a scope judgment wrong. My mild preference is to include it, because a note that asserts the reverse of the code is the same class of harm as the three reference pages I am already fixing — but it is your call and I will do either without argument.\n\nFINDING TWO. A REAL BEHAVIOURAL CONSEQUENCE, WHICH THE REVIEWER SHOULD SEE STATED PLAINLY RATHER THAN DISCOVER.\n\nA repository carrying another workroom's advertisement — the exact multi-workroom case that note analyses — now refuses every gs command until the operator removes the record or passes --server -. That is the intended fail-closed outcome and I believe it is right: acting locally because someone else's resident wrote the file first is precisely the silent whole-log rebuild the request exists to stop. But it converts a previously-working degraded mode into a hard stop for anyone in that situation, and calling that \"no behaviour change\" would be false. It is a behaviour change, it is deliberate, and it makes the per-genesis advertisement that note proposes more urgent rather than less.\n\nWHAT THE HEAD CONTAINS, IN SHORT.\n\nThe tri-state read replaces Workspace.ResidentURL rather than sitting beside it — the boolean is gone, so there is nothing left for the next caller to collapse. Only a genuinely missing file counts as absence; unreadable, oversized, unparseable, truncated, empty-address and foreign-genesis all carry their reason. The read is bounded at 8 KiB. resolveServerURL turns the third state into a refusal naming the failure and offering --server -, and it already ran before key custody in all six write commands, which is now pinned by a test that fails if a key read is moved ahead of it. cmd/gitseq-mcp keeps its behaviour, but as a written adapter decision with the reason, not as an inherited bool.\n\nTen classes plus the dead-address dial are covered, each asserting its OWN guard's reason string. That last detail is load-bearing and worth stating because it is exactly the trap I fell into last time: with the resolver's fail-closed branch deleted, five of those classes STILL produce an error, because ValidateURL catches the fallthrough with different words. A suite that only asserted \"it refused\" would have passed with the actual